<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chikka Devaraja Wodeyar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1645">1645</a>–<a href="/years/1704">1704</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chikka <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/devaraja">Devaraja</a> Wodeyar was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysore">Mysore</a> from 1673 to 1704. He expanded the kingdom across southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a>, bought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengaluru">Bangalore</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a> in 1689 and organised the administration into eighteen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">departments</a>. He also reformed revenue collection and was a patron of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannada">Kannada</a> literature.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chikka-devaraja-wodeyar.jpg" alt="Chikka Devaraja Wodeyar" width="320" height="447" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Artist unknown, photographer unknown, author: C. Hyavadana Rao · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chikkadevaraja.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Chikka <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/devaraja">Devaraja</a> was born on 22 September 1645, the eldest son of Maharani Amrit Ammani and Dodda Deva Raja, elder brother of Devaraja Wodeyar I and governor of a town of the kingdom. He succeeded his uncle, who died on 11 February 1673, and was installed on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysore">Mysore</a> throne on 28 February 1673 as the fourteenth maharaja.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>He took Maddagiri, which brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysore">Mysore</a> up to the Carnatic-Bijapur-Balaghat province held by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ekoji-i">Venkoji</a>, the Raja of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thanjavur">Tanjore</a> and half-brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shivaji">Shivaji</a>. In his first decade he imposed many petty taxes on the peasants, from which his soldiers were exempt, and the burden provoked wide protests among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ryot">ryots</a>, backed by the Jangama priests of the Virasaiva monasteries. Accounts say the king invited over 400 priests to a feast at Nanjanagudu and had his wrestlers strangle each as he left through a narrow lane, which ended the protests.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a> made Carnatic-Bijapur-Balaghat part of their province of Sira, Chikka Devaraja cultivated an alliance with Aurangzeb through Qasim Khan, the Mughal faujdar of Sira. He annexed Baramahal and Salem below the Eastern Ghats, extended his lands west to the Baba Budan Mountains in 1694, and two years later attacked the Naiks of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madura">Madura</a> and besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiruchirappalli">Trichinopoly</a>, a siege abandoned as his alliances began to break.</p><p>After Qasim Khan's death he sent an embassy to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmadnagar">Ahmadnagar</a>, and in 1700 the emperor sent him a signet ring bearing the title Jug Deo Raj, leave to sit on an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> throne and a sword from his own regalia. The embassy also meant formal subordination to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a> and an annual tribute, and his new administration is said to have imitated what the envoys had seen at the Mughal court.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>When he died on 16 November 1704 his lands reached from Medigeshi in the north to Palni and Anaimalai in the south. Subrahmanyam judged the state he left at once strong and weak, its growth resting on alliances that undermined its stability. A devout Śrīvaiṣṇava, he wrote the Gītagōpālam, a musical work on the life of Kṛṣṇa, and the Cikadēvarāya Binnapaṃ, a theological work in verse.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Chikka Devaraja” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
